      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "### What happened?\r\n\r\nCREATE request for already existing object is denied with `exceeded quota` by the ResourceQuota admission plugin.\r\n\r\n### What did you expect to happen?\r\n\r\nCREATE request for already existing object (and when the resourcequota is exhausted) to be denied with `already exists` (`409 Conflict`).\r\n\r\n### How can we reproduce it (as minimally and precisely as possible)?\r\n\r\n1. Create a test namespace `foo`\r\n   ```\r\n   k create ns foo\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n2. Create a resourcequota that allows only 1 Pod.\r\n   ```yaml\r\n   apiVersion: v1\r\n   kind: ResourceQuota\r\n   metadata:\r\n     name: resource-quota\r\n   spec:\r\n     hard:\r\n       pods: \"1\"\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n    ```\r\n    k -n foo create -f resource-qouta.yaml\r\n    ```\r\n\r\n3. Create a Pod\r\n\r\n   ```yaml\r\n   apiVersion: v1\r\n   kind: Pod\r\n   metadata:\r\n     name: nginx\r\n   spec:\r\n     containers:\r\n     - name: nginx\r\n       image: nginx:1.14.2\r\n       ports:\r\n       - containerPort: 80\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n   ```\r\n   k -n foo create -f pod.yaml\r\n    ```\r\n\r\n4. Try to create the Pod again and make sure that it fails with `exceeded quota`\r\n\r\n   ```\r\n   k -n foo create -f pod.yaml\r\n   Error from server (Forbidden): error when creating \"pod.yaml\": pods \"nginx\" is forbidden: exceeded quota: resource-quota, requested: pods=1, used: pods=1, limited: pods=1\r\n   ```\r\n\r\n### Anything else we need to know?\r\n\r\nOn server side we have an admission component (let's call it \"authorizer\") similar to the kubelet's [`NodeAuthorizer`](https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/node/) . The \"authorizer\" allows certain verbs on resources only if there is a \"relation\" between the requesting component and the requested resource - similar to the NodeAuthorizer that allows operations for secrets, configmaps, pvcs, pv related to pods bound to the kubelet's node. This authorizer would actually reject a GET request for non-existing object. That's why the client's strategy is \"create or get and patch\". We rely on the CREATE request to return `already exists` (`409 Conflict`).\r\n\r\n### Kubernetes version\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n\r\n```console\r\n$ kubectl version\r\nClient Version: version.Info{Major:\"1\", Minor:\"21\", GitVersion:\"v1.21.0\", GitCommit:\"cb303e613a121a29364f75cc67d3d580833a7479\", GitTreeState:\"clean\", BuildDate:\"2021-04-08T16:31:21Z\", GoVersion:\"go1.16.1\", Compiler:\"gc\", Platform:\"darwin/amd64\"}\r\nServer Version: version.Info{Major:\"1\", Minor:\"19\", GitVersion:\"v1.19.16\", GitCommit:\"e37e4ab4cc8dcda84f1344dda47a97bb1927d074\", GitTreeState:\"clean\", BuildDate:\"2021-10-27T16:20:18Z\", GoVersion:\"go1.15.15\", Compiler:\"gc\", Platform:\"linux/amd64\"}\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n\r\n### Cloud provider\r\n\r\n<details>\r\nNot applicable\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n\r\n### OS version\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n\r\n```console\r\n# On Linux:\r\n$ cat /etc/os-release\r\n# paste output here\r\n$ uname -a\r\n# paste output here\r\n\r\n# On Windows:\r\nC:\\> wmic os get Caption, Version, BuildNumber, OSArchitecture\r\n# paste output here\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n\r\n### Install tools\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n\r\n### Container runtime (CRI) and and version (if applicable)\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n\r\n### Related plugins (CNI, CSI, ...) and versions (if applicable)\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
